Our guest today is Ed Zitron, the founder and CEO of EZPR, a media relation company based in San Francisco.

10
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:44,880
Ed has worked with everyone fromone from drone maker Skydeo to game maker player unknown battlegrounds to app maker smart news.

11
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:50,640
He has a unique approach to media relations and is quite effective at helping his clients get results.

12
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:52,240
Welcome to the podcast, Ed.

13
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:53,600
Hey, thanks for having me.

14
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:54,480
Hi, Ed.

15
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:56,640
Hi, how's it going, Jacob?

16
00:00:56,960 --> 00:00:57,280
It's good.

17
00:00:57,760 --> 00:00:58,160
Good to talk.

18
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:00,400
It's good to always catch up.

19
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:02,480
I was just bothering you on Twitter.

20
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:08,480
David, you gave yourself away by calling it player unknown's battlegrounds instead of just PUBG.

21
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:13,200
Yeah, I'm don't play, not a gamer.

22
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:16,640
I worked for them and barely played it because I was extremely.

23
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:19,520
bad and I refused to help myself like that.

24
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:40,080
So yeah, Ed, I'm excited to learn a little bit more about what it is, the magic that you do, because we've been working, I've been working with you for a couple of years now, helping us with some RevenueCat PR, I've worked with Ed in the past, and I worked together in like 100 years ago.

25
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:44,720
Yeah, God, it was so much, so much different back then.

26
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:45,760
It was so different.

27
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:50,000
Yeah, you did like cut me a deal as a small indie app maker.

28
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,720
I remember you guys actually worked together.

29
00:01:52,880 --> 00:01:56,720
I paid you money back in like 20100 to pitch one of my app launches.

30
00:01:58,480 --> 00:01:59,920
That was so much easier back then.

31
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:01,200
Even then it was difficult.

32
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:03,760
I think let's start there.

33
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:11,360
Like, I had never known what PR we had worked at a company that hired a PR agency like a couple like ten, maybe 2013, 2011.

34
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:12,080
It was a disaster.

35
00:02:12,160 --> 00:02:13,280
Like, we paid them way too much money.

36
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:14,160
They got us nothing.

37
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:18,080
So from there, like, I was prepped with that experience.

38
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:21,440
But yeah, tell me, like, how did you get into this?

39
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:26,000
So the funny thing about how I got into PR is I was lied to.

40
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:28,160
So I was a journalist for a long time.

41
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:30,400
I was I started when I was like sixteen.

42
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:32,000
I moved to America when I was twenty two.

43
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:35,280
And I moved because I had read a bunch of PR books.

44
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:38,560
I hated England, hated living there, was miserable all the time.

45
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:40,960
Not that I'm not miserable now, but in a different manner.

46
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:48,000
And I read all these books and they made PR seem like it would be this big fancy thing with parties and press conferences.

47
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:50,800
Absolutely wrong, just complete falsehoods.

48
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:54,240
So I fell into my first PR job and I hated it.

49
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:55,120
I really did.

50
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:57,360
But I got very good with reporters.

51
00:02:57,920 --> 00:03:03,840
I got very good at talking to them about kind of working out what they wanted before and while talking to them.

52
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:10,640
And also, I was told by my bosses, I just have to keep calling them and harassing them basically until they give you what you want.

53
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:17,760
And I was like, well, that doesn't seem to work or be legal in any other realm of human existence.

54
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:24,160
So I'm going to try and I actually ended up working on an eReader, like one of the first it was like this, the one after Kindle.

55
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:25,280
It was not great.

56
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:26,240
No.

57
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:27,760
It was called The Cooler.

58
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:31,280
Oh, that's not the coolest cooler.

59
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:32,400
Yeah, that's it.

60
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:40,800
But I ended up, I was like, you know, I'm going to try something a bit different because usually my managers read all my pictures, but I was like, I don't care, I'm going to send it.

61
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:45,360
And I wrote a two or three line pitch just being like, it does this, here are the specs, what do you think?

62
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:47,840
And I got really, really good responses.

63
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:53,760
And I was like, maybe I'll try this again and maybe I'll read some of their stuff against all of the manager's advice.

64
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:55,600
They were like, just find names and spam them.

65
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,960
And I realized journalists generally want to hear about things they're interested in or their readers might be interested in.

66
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:02,720
It seems obvious, but it isn't.

67
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:05,600
And indeed, many PR people don't act that way.

68
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:20,720
And I went to another agency and I had a much better time there and I kind of grew my Rolodex and also got a lot more nuanced about how I actually build these relationships about how I'd follow these people and there'd be people I'd talk to and know for years that I wouldn't pitch.

69
00:04:21,280 --> 00:04:26,480
And then I'd get a bunch of stories with them eventually because you don't always have a client.

70
00:04:27,040 --> 00:04:28,160
for every reporter you have.

71
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:32,000
And I focused, honestly, much more on just making a lot of random friends.

72
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:35,040
So many of them online and it worked.

73
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:37,600
And yeah, and here we are.

74
00:04:37,760 --> 00:04:42,640
I started my own thing in 2012 and it has been a very different.

75
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:47,520
It's definitely a more challenging environment right now than it's ever been.

76
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:48,640
Oh, why?

77
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:49,360
What happened?

78
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:50,160
What changed?

79
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:51,840
So Facebook happened.

80
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:56,800
So around the time that I would say Theranos was kind of the first sign.

81
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:13,920
But when Facebook and all the Cambridge Analytica stuff came out, a lot of journalists who just acted like they had not been complicit in this, but there had been years and years and years of just effusive press for everything, especially Facebook.

82
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:21,600
The things that people would say about Facebook, even in 2013 with Prism, they were still standing for Facebook.

83
00:05:22,640 --> 00:05:33,360
I would say when that happened and also the dramatic shifts in like Indiegogo failures made the press a little more cynical and understandably so.

84
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:36,880
Oh, of just the industry, of anyone like just everything.

85
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:39,520
I feel that and it's not a Donald Trump thing.

86
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:45,120
I think it was just because 2014, 2015, you saw a lot of these individu Indiegogo things just crack.

87
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:47,360
Many of them just never happened.

88
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:49,280
Those that got delivered didn't look good.

89
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:54,560
The reason I picked that up specifically is that that was where a lot of the promise in tech was coming from.

90
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:57,040
You had that and you had a lot of letdowns.

91
00:05:57,680 --> 00:05:59,440
And then you had the Cambridge Analytica thing.

92
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:11,360
And I feel like that just really, that was a point at which the tech press became a lot more conservative about what they would be excited about, which is it's a generational thing.

93
00:06:11,680 --> 00:06:12,240
It was going to happen.

94
00:06:12,480 --> 00:06:14,160
Tech journalism is not particularly old.

95
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:20,640
I think like what, 80s, 90s, 90s was when they really started taking it seriously on a national level.

96
00:06:21,760 --> 00:06:23,680
So it's an adolescence thing.

97
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,040
It's just not of the age of the writers, but of the industry itself.

98
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:34,400
So that's a really great way to put it is that like early on in the app store, and you know, I've been building apps for thirteen years now.

99
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:37,120
I've been talking to press myself for thirteen years now.

100
00:06:37,920 --> 00:06:58,960
And I have noticed this kind of shift from like in those early days, and I hadn't thought about it in that kind of framework, but it was really around that time where you kind of went from this like tech press that was kind of excited about technology and would write kind of like fan-friendly.

101
00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:01,520
Like fanboy kind of like, hey, like the iPhone's fun.

102
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:03,040
Like this is changing our lives.

103
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:04,720
Like these apps are really exciting.

104
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:16,800
To then there started to be this shift where it's like, um, and, and some of it, and maybe that, that, that adolescent thing is like a combination of both, um, some level of maturing.

105
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:24,720
It's like, well, we, we as journalists, you know, it used to be more like, kind of tech bloggers and they saw themselves not necessarily like journalists.

106
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:26,080
And that started to shift.

107
00:07:26,240 --> 00:07:27,280
Like, okay, we're journalists.

108
00:07:27,440 --> 00:07:30,240
We need to kind of like talk about the good and the bad and stuff like that.

109
00:07:30,400 --> 00:07:41,600
But then there's also that like maybe teenage angst and cynicism mixed in., like, all of a sudden it was like, how many favors we did these people in the, like, you know, middle of.

110
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:44,560
Yeah, but it's like, apps are dead.

111
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:47,120
Like, apps suck, tech sucks.

112
00:07:47,280 --> 00:07:48,560
Like, tech is ruining our lives.

113
00:07:48,640 --> 00:07:52,800
There, there, there seems to be that, like, the narrative started to shift around that time.

114
00:07:53,200 --> 00:08:04,000
And so, yeah, it's really interesting, I've never kind of put that together, but it has been so different, even specifically talking about apps over the last few years than it was early on.

115
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:07,680
Like, there was kind of that excitement about technology and excitement about things.

116
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:09,600
And it's definitely shifted.

117
00:08:09,680 --> 00:08:11,600
That's, yeah, that's an interesting perspective.

118
00:08:12,160 --> 00:08:14,240
And there's a froth that also existed.

119
00:08:14,880 --> 00:08:19,360
I think it is also, and I say this loving TechCrunch, I've read it for years.

120
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:28,880
I think that TechCrunch and others, but TechCrunch in particular, started off honestly with, like, I don't think there was anything malevolent going on.

121
00:08:29,200 --> 00:08:31,440
It was started off by Arrington and he was excited.

122
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:35,120
Yeah, there was a bit of a kind of muckracking where he was like, oh, I found the scoop.

123
00:08:35,440 --> 00:08:36,400
But there was an excitement.

124
00:08:36,720 --> 00:08:38,480
And I get that people were carried away in that.

125
00:08:38,880 --> 00:08:51,280
I also think there were people like Josh Constein at TechCrunch who wrote effusively about Facebook because they were genuinely interested, which kind of made people look a bit silly when Facebook turned out to be extremely bad.

126
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:54,080
Well, I was going to say, it just stuff got big, right?

127
00:08:54,240 --> 00:08:55,120
The power dynamics changed.

128
00:08:55,280 --> 00:08:55,440
It did.

129
00:08:55,920 --> 00:08:57,040
And I think that that's really it.

130
00:08:58,320 --> 00:09:09,440
It went from being an enthusiast press to an industry press, which, weirdly enough, that happened quicker for tech, sorry, slower for tech than it happened for games.

131
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:14,080
Like games still hasn't quite made it, despite really being more grand.

132
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:16,720
Gaming is still enthusiast.

133
00:09:16,960 --> 00:09:20,320
And also I think that that's a fanboy press thing, but irrelevant to apps.

134
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:23,840
But that used to be the case in tech more broadly.

135
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,000
It was that kind of fanboy excited about tech.

136
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:26,400
Yeah.

137
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:29,040
I mean specifically, I see what you say about games too.

138
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:29,280
Yeah.

139
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:29,920
Oh, God.

140
00:09:30,080 --> 00:09:35,200
But those embarrassing at people like hooting and hollering at a press event for Apple, come on.

141
00:09:35,520 --> 00:09:40,480
I mean, I'm excited about new stuff too, but please calm your jaws.

142
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:41,360
We don't need you to.

143
00:09:41,920 --> 00:09:42,800
It's going to be different now.

144
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:45,840
Like when I was doing it, it's still the same.

145
00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:47,440
They know who to invite.

146
00:09:47,680 --> 00:09:55,920
And actually, I think if you look at how Apple has worked with the press, the last five years, you can really see they are much more aware of this stuff than even the tech press were.

147
00:09:56,160 --> 00:10:08,160
I forget which iPhone, like two or three back, there was a Gruber went completely nuts because they gave it to high snobbery and a few other more mainstream consumer and like a YouTuber, I think.

148
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:12,240
And I remember people are like, why didn't they give it to Gruber?

149
00:10:12,560 --> 00:10:17,600
The answer is Apple is aware that industry is going to be a little more critical now.

150
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:25,600
Despite being a $100 billion company before and now a trillion dollar company now, I think that it's an adjustment.

151
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:36,880
It doesn't mean that it's hopeless doing PR, otherwise I wouldn't be here, but it would, it's no longer a turnkey thing of just, yeah, this is, this is that easy.

152
00:10:37,200 --> 00:10:38,960
It's like that for funding press now.

153
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:46,160
And I don't think that's ever going to change because yet PR people still screw that up, which we'll get to later.

154
00:10:46,960 --> 00:10:52,960
But I think it's interesting to see how tech has had to become predominantly industry press.

155
00:10:53,200 --> 00:10:54,480
Now it was enthusiasts.

156
00:10:54,640 --> 00:10:59,360
There was a time where even the New York Times was basically enthusiast tech.

157
00:11:00,240 --> 00:11:02,640
People don't really think about that, but like Was it?

158
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:04,960
No, Mossberg was at the Journal.

159
00:11:05,200 --> 00:11:06,320
Mossberg was at the Journal.

160
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:08,400
But even his writing then.

161
00:11:09,280 --> 00:11:10,400
Oh, he's at the Times.

162
00:11:10,560 --> 00:11:10,880
Oh, Poe.

163
00:11:11,040 --> 00:11:11,360
That's cool.

164
00:11:12,720 --> 00:11:13,280
I love Poe.

165
00:11:13,360 --> 00:11:15,840
And I think But as the enthusiast, like, yeah.

166
00:11:16,400 --> 00:11:16,800
That was great.

167
00:11:16,960 --> 00:11:18,320
But that's what the Times needs.

168
00:11:18,560 --> 00:11:21,600
The Times needs your weird uncle who makes his weird songs.

169
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:35,600
But I think Joanna Stern at the Journal, who used to be a she was a founding member of The Verge, was at N Gadget, and then I think ABC before that, she has picked up what David Pogue did and run with it.

170
00:11:36,640 --> 00:11:56,320
So she is probably the best, she may be the best tech journalist actually working today, just because she is so talented and so astute and yet able to entertainingly say, here is what this means and why you should care, in but also adopting some of the kind of joyful enthusiasm that Pogue had.

171
00:11:56,640 --> 00:11:59,680
When she finds something she loves, she loves it and it's great.

172
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,720
And I feel like tech journalism needs more of that even putting aside.

173
00:12:03,280 --> 00:12:11,200
my own job, like, it is a lot of fun watching someone whose job it is to be critical genuinely won over by something.

174
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:12,000
Yeah.

175
00:12:12,240 --> 00:12:16,720
Adita Bone over at the verge, his, uh, one of his M one reviews.

176
00:12:17,040 --> 00:12:20,160
It was great reading it because he wasn't not being critical.

177
00:12:20,400 --> 00:12:22,400
Apple just exceeded his expectations.

178
00:12:22,960 --> 00:12:23,840
I think that's great.

179
00:12:24,320 --> 00:12:28,640
I think a lot of tech people get obsessed with the minutiae.

180
00:12:29,040 --> 00:12:31,040
Like, well, I need to be a little more critical.

181
00:12:31,280 --> 00:12:34,400
The truth is, most people don't view things like that.

182
00:12:35,680 --> 00:12:35,840
Yeah.

183
00:12:36,240 --> 00:12:38,480
I'm backing up a little bit.

184
00:12:38,720 --> 00:12:39,040
Like, what?

185
00:12:39,680 --> 00:13:03,520
I think one of the things that I've had to come around to and just like understanding this, this whole industry, like when you're working with app creators or, or, or, I don't know, firms of varying size, like what, what, what are the, I don't know, when you're thinking about getting a placement or getting to one of these people to write about, I mean, you know, enthusiastic or not, like, what are you thinking about optimizing for?

186
00:13:03,760 --> 00:13:09,280
Like when you're, when you're thinking about, like, I mean, I'm thinking about our listeners here, you know, we have a lot of folks who are building apps.

187
00:13:09,600 --> 00:13:18,640
They probably, I mean, David, I don't know if the days are gone of like you would just be sending out cold emails to, I mean, in 2008 there were like 202 apps or something like that.

188
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:21,040
I don't think the days are gone.

189
00:13:21,920 --> 00:13:25,680
I think that it comes down to the utility of the app being truly unique.

190
00:13:26,480 --> 00:13:26,560
Okay.

191
00:13:27,040 --> 00:13:39,920
Because I will get email introductions or cold emails to work on apps that are doing some form of social network and the thrust of it is always, well, it's like this but a bit different.

192
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:46,160
The problem with any social product like social apps for the most part are god awful to pitch.

193
00:13:46,400 --> 00:13:54,160
There's just no differentiation unless it's the founding team of X, Y, Z, Google thing or Facebook person.

194
00:13:54,480 --> 00:13:56,160
Then even then that becomes a business story.

195
00:13:56,880 --> 00:14:00,160
If your app, for example, I work with an app called Do Not Pay right now.

196
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:01,200
I love these guys.

197
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:03,840
They also come up with a new product like every week.

198
00:14:04,080 --> 00:14:05,120
It's truly amazing.

199
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:08,720
But also with Do Not Pay, it's an app with a subscription.

200
00:14:08,880 --> 00:14:10,240
It's three dollars a month.

201
00:14:10,800 --> 00:14:19,280
And with them, their big thing is they are meant to be a robot lawyer and they have features that are built regularly to pitch.

202
00:14:19,520 --> 00:14:20,560
They have regular updates.

203
00:14:21,600 --> 00:14:25,280
As a larger thing, if there were no updates, that would be quite a difficult pitch.

204
00:14:25,680 --> 00:14:30,800
And yes, there are industry wide things like why I just ran like a six apps for managing subscriptions.

205
00:14:31,040 --> 00:14:31,360
Perfect.

206
00:14:32,480 --> 00:14:33,680
There are not many of those a year.

207
00:14:34,400 --> 00:14:38,480
Now, if your app really does something that most people will find useful, brilliant.

208
00:14:38,640 --> 00:14:40,240
You have a utility, that's great.

209
00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:48,720
But the problem is finding that match between both the utility for people and the reporters who are going to give a damn about said utility.

210
00:14:49,440 --> 00:14:58,400
So you are constantly finding this line with new clients and in case an app developer, if you're working with a PR agency, go month to month.

211
00:14:58,560 --> 00:15:02,880
First of all, sign on for like, don't sign on for multiple months.

212
00:15:03,200 --> 00:15:03,920
Just don't do it.

213
00:15:05,280 --> 00:15:10,480
But also, I would argue you want to make sure that there are journalists writing about this sort of thing.

214
00:15:11,040 --> 00:15:23,440
There are actually some really good sites like iMore, reviewgeek, android police, all places that are giving app developers real time of day, the time of day even, I can speak, I promise.

215
00:15:24,560 --> 00:15:29,200
Like there are journalists there who genuinely are both critics and enthusiasts.

216
00:15:29,520 --> 00:15:30,960
David Ruddock, he's bloody brilliant.

217
00:15:31,120 --> 00:15:34,080
You've got Ryan Hager over at Android police, he's fantastic.

218
00:15:34,240 --> 00:15:38,880
Cam Summerson over at reviewgeek is one of the nicest people, but also genuinely knows his stuff.

219
00:15:39,440 --> 00:15:44,400
And I feel like the you've had the pinch of kind of cynicism.

220
00:15:44,720 --> 00:15:47,280
You really saw that in 2018 and 2019.

221
00:15:47,920 --> 00:15:54,640
In fact, I'd argue 2019 was the hardest year of my business since like 2012 or the year of my divorce in 2014.

222
00:15:55,200 --> 00:16:08,320
Like I mean, and even then still easy, like 2019 sucked because I think 2019 was the height of cynicism with everything going on socioeconomically on top of just the press being so pissed off.

223
00:16:09,520 --> 00:16:24,800
And I think it's kind of diffused now, but also the enthusiasts have kind of worked out because there is I would argue there is more space for enthusiast press than there is for business and industry press because deep down people want to know the crap to buy.

224
00:16:25,040 --> 00:16:38,720
They want to know what app to buy, what to download, but as an app developer., there's nothing wrong with you just reading a whole bunch of stuff and emailing the reporter with 98 or 110 words and saying, This is my app.

225
00:16:38,960 --> 00:16:39,520
This is what it does.

226
00:16:39,680 --> 00:16:40,480
This is why it's good.

227
00:16:40,720 --> 00:16:42,240
Don't use high-voluting terms.

228
00:16:42,400 --> 00:16:43,760
Don't be like, It's amazing.

229
00:16:44,160 --> 00:16:45,600
It's evolutionary.

230
00:16:46,160 --> 00:16:46,960
Nobody cares.

231
00:16:47,680 --> 00:16:51,360
But also, not just nobody cares, but those things actually tell you nothing.

232
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:54,640
Subjective language is not illustrative.

233
00:16:55,520 --> 00:16:55,760
Right.

234
00:16:56,160 --> 00:16:57,040
It really isn't.

235
00:16:57,200 --> 00:17:01,840
It's like saying something is amazing is subjective at its core.

236
00:17:02,160 --> 00:17:07,680
Like a caveman would find even the crappiest app amazing because they're not a caveman.

237
00:17:07,840 --> 00:17:08,720
Don't know how they got here.

238
00:17:08,880 --> 00:17:09,520
That's up to you.

239
00:17:10,560 --> 00:17:14,240
In all seriousness, what is amazing to you isn't amazing to someone else.

240
00:17:14,480 --> 00:17:20,960
What is, and frankly, the big secret of PR is that every journalist pretty much tells you what they want.

241
00:17:21,200 --> 00:17:24,800
If you go on Twitter and read their tweets and on their articles, you read their stuff.

242
00:17:25,200 --> 00:17:27,520
The difficult ones are the ones who write once a month.

243
00:17:27,760 --> 00:17:29,120
Then you just really need to know them.

244
00:17:29,680 --> 00:17:34,080
But if you want someone to write about your app, read their stuff repeatedly.

245
00:17:34,480 --> 00:17:37,600
There are quite difficult ones to get to, like Sarah Perez at TechCrunch.

246
00:17:38,240 --> 00:17:40,640
She writes a lot of general app stuff.

247
00:17:40,800 --> 00:17:44,800
She also writes about industry stuff like Sense Tower and her work together quite regularly.

248
00:17:45,120 --> 00:17:48,640
Now she's written some revenue cap related things with her.

249
00:17:48,960 --> 00:17:57,200
She will write about apps, but you also have to realize that that's probably 1000 to 2000 emails a day going to one woman in Florida.

250
00:17:58,720 --> 00:18:07,120
Even if you write the best email in the world, it might still get ignored and that's not you offending her or her disliking you or her being bad.

251
00:18:07,360 --> 00:18:09,360
It's just it's not her job to write about you.

252
00:18:09,520 --> 00:18:11,280
It's her job to write about something.

253
00:18:12,160 --> 00:18:16,160
And that's where I think a lot of people miss, especially app developers.

254
00:18:16,400 --> 00:18:18,720
And like I talk to a lot, I do office hours every week.

255
00:18:18,960 --> 00:18:22,000
I've talked to, you know, thousands of developers in the community over the years.

256
00:18:23,360 --> 00:18:25,440
And I have gotten a lot of press for my apps over the years.

257
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:28,240
So I get a lot of questions like, you know, how do you do that?

258
00:18:28,400 --> 00:18:29,200
You know, what's the secret?

259
00:18:30,240 --> 00:18:31,760
And it's exactly what you're saying.

260
00:18:31,920 --> 00:18:47,760
And I wanted to kind of like dive a little bit deeper into some of what you just said but didn't go deep on is that a journalist, you have to think about their motivation, their, you know, what their job is.

261
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:49,680
Their job is to inform readers.

262
00:18:50,240 --> 00:18:53,520
And then, I mean, cynically at some point, it's also to get clicks.

263
00:18:53,760 --> 00:19:00,640
So like there is an aspect to just understanding their business model, why they write, who they write to.

264
00:19:01,120 --> 00:19:09,040
And so when you're thinking like, oh, I'm going to pitch my app, you know, you see very niche apps wanting to get press in TechCrunch.

265
00:19:09,120 --> 00:19:14,160
And it's like, there's no reason for TechCrunch to write about your niche app.

266
00:19:14,560 --> 00:19:16,080
Like, go look for the niche.

267
00:19:16,640 --> 00:19:20,080
There is a third thing to add to that though, because you're completely correct.

268
00:19:20,880 --> 00:19:24,400
But there's also a third thing, their own interests.

269
00:19:25,120 --> 00:19:31,520
So TechCrunch, Devin Coldaway, I worked with him on and it was a computer version product.

270
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:37,680
The reason I did that was he had written specifically about the Myriad chipset that was in that thing.

271
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,800
So I was able to go to him and go, you are interested in this.

272
00:19:41,040 --> 00:19:42,800
You know what this is and why this matters.

273
00:19:43,440 --> 00:19:44,880
What do you think about chatting with them?

274
00:19:45,120 --> 00:19:46,320
And he had a chat with them.

275
00:19:46,720 --> 00:19:52,160
So you might read someone's stuff and pitch them and they won't care.

276
00:19:52,400 --> 00:20:00,880
But you might also see they genuinely interest in something or they're the only person who wrote about this thing and they may read it and go, Oh, that's actually personally interesting.

277
00:20:01,040 --> 00:20:01,680
Because guess what?

278
00:20:02,320 --> 00:20:03,680
Everything David said is true.

279
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:08,320
And they have to couch that with not being particularly thrilled with having to get clicks.

280
00:20:09,040 --> 00:20:14,000
If they can find a way of molding their own interest, the reader interest in the clicks, that's magical.

281
00:20:14,240 --> 00:20:14,960
That makes them happy.

282
00:20:15,040 --> 00:20:20,400
That makes their life easier not on a professional level but on a personal level as well.

283
00:20:20,880 --> 00:20:22,480
Yeah, that's a really great point.

284
00:20:22,720 --> 00:20:22,800
Yeah.

285
00:20:23,440 --> 00:20:23,920
And it works.

286
00:20:24,080 --> 00:20:28,960
And if you can find also a journalist who would genuinely use the product, even better.

287
00:20:29,280 --> 00:20:32,480
When I was working with SkyDeO, I really dug deep.

288
00:20:32,640 --> 00:20:36,960
It would have been easy just, yeah, sure, we did look for everyone who covered the first SkyDeO.

289
00:20:37,120 --> 00:20:37,280
Sure.

290
00:20:37,840 --> 00:20:42,640
But we went and found people who were genuinely interested in photography.

291
00:20:43,680 --> 00:20:46,000
Chris Davies over at SlashGate, wonderful fellow.

292
00:20:46,880 --> 00:20:48,880
He is an avid car bloke.

293
00:20:49,200 --> 00:20:54,720
And of course, we had footage around cars and following cars, like very simple stuff like.

294
00:20:55,120 --> 00:20:59,840
that is very obvious when you read a little bit, spend a lot more time reading than you do pitching.

295
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:00,560
And it's tough.

296
00:21:00,640 --> 00:21:01,040
I get it.

297
00:21:01,200 --> 00:21:03,440
You're building your app at the same time as doing all this.

298
00:21:04,320 --> 00:21:10,640
That's why you might hire an agency, but you have to realize, and this is something I know we're going to bridge into, what are you actually spending your money on?

299
00:21:10,800 --> 00:21:13,920
You're trying to get clicks, you're trying to get installs, you're trying to build a brand.

300
00:21:14,640 --> 00:21:19,920
Be very clear about that because PR is not an efficient, it's not a CPA.

301
00:21:20,720 --> 00:21:21,200
It can be.

302
00:21:21,280 --> 00:21:22,320
Well, you can't even measure it.

303
00:21:23,200 --> 00:21:24,400
Let's talk about that for a little bit.

304
00:21:24,720 --> 00:21:35,360
So like if, if, you know, if, if someone goes to a PR firm saying, we're going to spend ten thousand dollars this month and we want to see a $20,000 return on investment.

305
00:21:35,920 --> 00:21:40,800
What's your conversation with a potential client that comes to you and says, here's our budget.

306
00:21:41,200 --> 00:21:42,800
Here's the return we want to see on that budget.

307
00:21:43,120 --> 00:21:43,440
I do this.

308
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:44,480
I go, no.

309
00:21:49,360 --> 00:21:53,360
I will usually say to them, that is not how PR works.

310
00:21:53,840 --> 00:21:57,280
It can be, but it is not regularly.

311
00:21:57,840 --> 00:22:01,600
Like there was, I forget what, it was her VPN client I worked on.

312
00:22:02,480 --> 00:22:07,360
I think they had like a USA Today story that ended up making them like a million dollars over a few years.

313
00:22:07,680 --> 00:22:08,320
Like, wow.

314
00:22:08,560 --> 00:22:12,000
Then there are articles they got in TechCrunch'scription like three installs.

315
00:22:12,560 --> 00:22:13,840
It's very hard.

316
00:22:14,400 --> 00:22:19,920
I have a client who had a client even last year who does a privacy software.

317
00:22:20,480 --> 00:22:22,800
It's kind of a long explanation.

318
00:22:23,200 --> 00:22:26,800
They got a Forbes story and I think it made them like three thousand dollars in one week.

319
00:22:27,760 --> 00:22:32,400
Like there are examples you can make, but there are no specific ones.

320
00:22:32,720 --> 00:22:35,840
And I think you should just not do PR if that's your goal.

321
00:22:36,320 --> 00:22:39,840
I think this is true for like any kind of I mean, PR is a growth experiment, right?

322
00:22:40,080 --> 00:22:41,040
Like it may work, it may not.

323
00:22:41,440 --> 00:22:42,560
It's a force multiplier.

324
00:22:43,040 --> 00:22:44,240
It can't be the only thing you do.

325
00:22:44,560 --> 00:22:46,160
I mean, actually, I was going to ask David about this.

326
00:22:46,320 --> 00:22:47,200
Like, David, have you?

327
00:22:47,360 --> 00:22:54,000
Because I remember in the early days., it seemed like it was much more of a achievable, certainly without a PR firm, right?

328
00:22:54,240 --> 00:22:55,360
Like you could kind of like email.

329
00:22:55,680 --> 00:23:03,200
I remember in 2008 working on little projects and stuff, and we would just like email people at TechCrunch and you might probably, if you needed Yeah, you might break through.

330
00:23:03,520 --> 00:23:04,640
Yeah, you can just get through.

331
00:23:06,080 --> 00:23:08,800
But it seems like it, you know, it's just different now.

332
00:23:09,040 --> 00:23:20,000
So as like a when you think about this channel, like, is there a stage when you think it's good to start thinking about PR or, you know, maybe it's not the first thing you jump to, right?

333
00:23:20,320 --> 00:23:33,120
Well, here's something I've been talking a lot about with developers in my office hours and otherwise is understanding like cold pitches can still work.

334
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:37,040
And working with Ed is kind of a different level.

335
00:23:37,280 --> 00:23:51,920
So when you're a small bootstrapped app trying to get some attention, what I've been telling people is, and this is exactly what Ed was kind of talking about, is like you can get in TechCrunch and you think, oh my gosh, like we're in TechCrunch.

336
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:53,120
This is going to make us a million dollars.

337
00:23:53,280 --> 00:23:53,680
This is it.

338
00:23:53,760 --> 00:23:55,120
We're going to get millions of downloads.

339
00:23:55,280 --> 00:23:56,640
And I've seen this with my own apps.

340
00:23:56,800 --> 00:24:04,160
Like I've gotten tons of press at the wrong time with the wrong feature, with the wrong, you know, and I get, I make like a couple of grand.

341
00:24:04,240 --> 00:24:48,480
grand and like it's just not even worth it but then and this is a great example I should I need to follow up and get details on this but a buddy of mine Andy runs an RV app and and he he emails me one day he's like how do you track how do you figure out sources of downloads because I got all these downloads all of a sudden and I have no idea where they came from yeah you're trying to work it out so he tracks it back and it was like a small time youtuber but that youtuber had a really dedicated audience that was super into it and this youtuber organically picked up the app give an effusive enthusiast you know tech person review and the conversion from watching the video to downloading the app was off the charts.

342
00:24:49,200 --> 00:25:02,640
So I think when you're early on, the best thing to do is go look for those niche publications, niche YouTubers, niche sources of attention that are really so deep into your target market.

343
00:25:03,280 --> 00:25:07,600
And that's going to be 10 times more effective than cold emailing TechCrunch.

344
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:14,240
And then as you grow and have a broader market app, then you can start thinking about working with Ed.

345
00:25:14,880 --> 00:25:16,640
And then Ed, you take it from there.

346
00:25:16,880 --> 00:25:18,800
So now we've got from that small thing.

347
00:25:19,360 --> 00:25:20,480
Now, at what stage do you think?

348
00:25:21,280 --> 00:25:24,640
people should come talk to you and think about PR more broadly?

349
00:25:25,200 --> 00:25:29,040
What's somebody that you would talk to you and you'd be like, no, this is not like it's too early.

350
00:25:29,200 --> 00:25:30,720
This is not the right time to think about this.

351
00:25:31,520 --> 00:25:45,760
Honestly, the money side is part of it, not just because like, hey, we have prices, but also because if whatever we cost, like it, but to an agency, it can cost between like 8,000 and 25,000 a month.

352
00:25:46,240 --> 00:25:51,040
If you look at that and that it's going to be make or break money for your business, it's the wrong time to do PR.

353
00:25:51,440 --> 00:25:52,000
Just don't do it.

354
00:25:52,160 --> 00:25:52,560
Don't pay.

355
00:25:52,720 --> 00:25:59,520
Like if that's, if you can't afford to lose it, obviously you should not be hiring someone who's going to just do nothing.

356
00:25:59,840 --> 00:26:10,080
But if you can't afford to like, spend that money and not like, and you need that routine, you must have eight thousand click throughs or what have you, then yeah, it's the wrong time.

357
00:26:10,320 --> 00:26:19,200
Also, something I want to add for a forget with your emails to journalists, if you're doing this yourself, this is something that so many people forget, make it very clear what you want out of them.

358
00:26:19,760 --> 00:26:21,360
So, and make it very easy for them.

359
00:26:21,520 --> 00:26:28,320
So, if you're not hiring an agency, you're sending these emails yourself, 90 to 110 words feature set, nice bullet point list, brilliant.

360
00:26:28,640 --> 00:26:31,840
At the end, have a link to the app on iOS and Android and have a media kit.

361
00:26:32,160 --> 00:26:35,040
Media kit can just be screenshots, a little fact sheet.

362
00:26:35,600 --> 00:26:36,560
A hero is huge.

363
00:26:37,520 --> 00:26:41,760
because the heroes make it so much easier to get picked up because Can you define a hero, David?

364
00:26:42,240 --> 00:26:47,040
Oh, just like a splashy image that has something to do with your app.

365
00:26:47,360 --> 00:26:48,880
An image that would look good on an article.

366
00:26:49,200 --> 00:26:55,520
Yeah, when stories get shared on Twitter, the hero gets pulled in by the Twitter, I forgot what they call the image tag thing.

367
00:26:55,840 --> 00:27:02,720
And so when you have a nice image that's really easy for them to drop in a story, that just makes it so much easier to cover.

368
00:27:03,040 --> 00:27:10,160
So your media kit should have screenshots, but actually I think more important than the screenshots is actually a hero as part of the media kit.

369
00:27:10,720 --> 00:27:13,360
And ultimately you should just be making it easy for them.

370
00:27:13,680 --> 00:27:14,480
They should be able to go.

371
00:27:15,280 --> 00:27:16,240
Oh, that's interesting.

372
00:27:16,560 --> 00:27:17,680
I'm going to download that now.

373
00:27:17,920 --> 00:27:18,640
Oh, I have a question.

374
00:27:19,040 --> 00:27:19,920
There's a fact sheet.

375
00:27:20,640 --> 00:27:24,880
And this goes also with your PR agency, but they should do that themselves.

376
00:27:25,760 --> 00:27:37,840
Also, you should hire a PR agency if you truly, deep in your heart of hearts, in your rotten little soul, you think you have something truly crazy that will go wild.

377
00:27:39,120 --> 00:27:43,040
I have had people who believe that, who I have informed were not correct.

378
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:45,760
And I have been correct at that time.

379
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:51,920
I do not have a loss on the you are you are drinking your own cool late there.

380
00:27:52,960 --> 00:27:54,640
But I've had a few have come to me.

381
00:27:55,040 --> 00:28:08,960
I mean, I'm trying to think like Scudio would be an obvious one, but there have been numerous times where like Wise, when I worked with Wise right at the beginning, and they came to me, I priced to compete because I was like, this is going to be a $20 WiFi camera.

382
00:28:09,120 --> 00:28:10,000
It's going to go nuts.

383
00:28:10,080 --> 00:28:10,960
It's the year 2018.

384
00:28:11,280 --> 00:28:12,320
It's going to go bonkers.

385
00:28:12,640 --> 00:28:12,960
And it did.

386
00:28:13,840 --> 00:28:19,760
When someone can, when you, when you have something like that, we're like, I really think this is going to be big, but I on my own cannot do this.

387
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:26,880
It is worth rolling the dice because if you, you on your own are going to have a much harder time doing it.

388
00:28:27,680 --> 00:28:30,320
And even if you have investors who are like, oh, I know that guy.

389
00:28:30,400 --> 00:28:30,720
I know that guy.

390
00:28:30,800 --> 00:28:36,880
I know, like most of the time, the person that they know is like one person at TechCrunch, one person at Bloomberg.

391
00:28:37,040 --> 00:28:40,400
The person at Bloomberg is going to say, You're not big enough yet and you're going to get nothing.

392
00:28:41,280 --> 00:28:52,240
You actually really want, and to David's point, you really want a cluster of smaller hits with people who are going to, and yeah, if it's a very value focused product, you're going to want TechCrunch.

393
00:28:52,880 --> 00:28:55,760
But more importantly, you're just going to want users who are going to talk about it.

394
00:28:56,000 --> 00:29:04,560
You want to make sure that people have something nice to share on Twitter, something that can talk about the product and why they love it.

395
00:29:05,200 --> 00:29:11,280
I think that's weird example., but there is this thermometer I have called the meter.

396
00:29:11,440 --> 00:29:13,120
It's a WiFi based thermometer.

397
00:29:13,600 --> 00:29:21,440
You go to take a screenshot of your cook as it's going on and it creates a nice perfectly formatted image very good for sharing.

398
00:29:22,640 --> 00:29:23,680
Stuff like that is magic.

399
00:29:23,760 --> 00:29:31,680
Obviously we're talking very vaguely here, but everything that you are doing should be around giving just as wide a spread as possible.

400
00:29:32,720 --> 00:29:36,000
I also want to add that does not mean paying for a press release.

401
00:29:36,240 --> 00:29:37,520
You do not need a press release.

402
00:29:38,080 --> 00:29:38,640
Stop asking.

403
00:29:39,840 --> 00:29:40,880
What is a press release?

404
00:29:41,360 --> 00:29:45,520
So right now, a press release is a really good way to waste $1,000 to $2,000.

405
00:29:47,440 --> 00:29:51,920
Are there still just a bunch of firms that will take your $1,000 as just their business model?

406
00:29:52,320 --> 00:29:54,720
PR news web, PR web, all of them.

407
00:29:54,880 --> 00:29:56,240
There might be a $400 example.

408
00:29:56,480 --> 00:29:57,360
There's no point of doing it.

409
00:29:57,520 --> 00:29:58,160
No one reads them.

410
00:29:58,480 --> 00:30:01,920
What they do is you'll be told you need a press release by someone.

411
00:30:02,800 --> 00:30:04,000
You will be asked for one.

412
00:30:04,640 --> 00:30:08,480
Whatever that press release does is better in a FAQ, better in a datasheet.

413
00:30:08,960 --> 00:30:18,000
Just like who you are, what you do, what you're doing, if you're raising money, how much you raised, who from, give them some quotes from investors, give them some quotes from you, done.

414
00:30:18,400 --> 00:30:19,840
You don't need to do a press release.

415
00:30:20,400 --> 00:30:21,680
There is no value to it.

416
00:30:23,040 --> 00:30:31,200
You want to if you want to do this on your your own, try and find as many good places to throw a big pebble into a river, try and get as many waves out.

417
00:30:31,920 --> 00:30:35,360
Finding you're not going to get seen by unbox therapy.

418
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:38,000
You might, but you probably won't.

